1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
*music*

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:55,600
*music*

3
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:58,920
Well, good morning, church. Good to see you again.

4
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:04,560
So glad that you have joined us as we continue this series through the book of Ruth.

5
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:10,040
We started two weeks ago with an overview of how the book works, and spoiler alert, I read the end.

6
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,960
So you already know where we're headed.

7
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:18,040
But we're gonna try and sit in the tension as we work our way through back to the ending that we already know is coming.

8
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:20,800
We started with an overview of how the book works.

9
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:27,840
It is a story, a literal story about individuals who went through tremendous suffering and yet saw God's redemption in their life.

10
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:33,480
But it's also a story about God's plan of redemption for the whole world.

11
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:41,120
It starts with the overwhelming suffering of one family and ends with the line of Jesus who brings about the redemption for all of creation.

12
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:48,760
I quoted one pastor who said the book of Ruth is the story of the whole Bible told in four chapters through three characters.

13
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:54,400
It's a story of God working out what seems to us to be a painstakingly slow process of redemption.

14
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:08,720
But remember we said that it might seem slow to us, but it's not slow to God, that God is patient, inviting all of his creation to seek comfort in his arms, to seek redemption through his process.

15
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:11,040
So it seems slow to us, but it's God's grace.

16
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:20,900
It's his generosity that makes his redemption so slow because he's wanting and waiting for more people to find comfort in his grace.

17
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:29,000
Last week, Kent did an excellent job of helping us understand culturally the level of suffering that Ruth and Naomi were experiencing.

18
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,680
They faced physical, emotional, spiritual, psychological suffering.

19
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:37,640
All of the suffering that any of us will face throughout our lives, they suffered all of it.

20
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:53,800
And he focused on how turning away from the things of this world, Ruth, turning her back to Moab, turning her back to her false gods, her false idols, the things that the world told her would find comfort and peace, and turning towards the God of the Old Testament, turning towards Yahweh.

21
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:59,880
It was like a seed planted in the ashes awaiting the redemption that God will ultimately have.

22
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:11,520
We intentionally chose the book of Ruth for the season of Lent because Lent is all about acknowledging, encountering, and voluntarily experiencing the suffering of this broken world.

23
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:18,660
As we look with anticipation towards the redemption that is fulfilled and secured on Easter.

24
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:25,240
We have the expressed privilege of living in a place where there are all four seasons, right?

25
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:29,820
And our whole city shuts down for a week called Apple Blossom.

26
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:30,680
You guys know this.

27
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:34,060
I avoid the city at all costs during that week.

28
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:35,760
I know many of you love to participate.

29
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:45,620
But it is this, it's this annual reminder that when a seed is planted in the ground and dies, it ultimately brings forth life.

30
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:48,220
And isn't that a picture of our spiritual lives?

31
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:53,020
If we're willing to lay our life down before the hands of the ultimate good father.

32
00:03:53,020 --> 00:04:00,840
And if we're willing to die to ourselves, to be planted in the ashes, that ultimately he will bring about his redemption and restoration.

33
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:07,540
So I hope throughout this series that we continue to sit in the suffering of our three main characters.

34
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:12,200
And that we begin to see God's slow working redemption plans in their lives.

35
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:17,640
I hope that it builds anticipation for us as we prepare to celebrate on Easter Sunday.

36
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:19,820
Now, I do have some good news.

37
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:25,980
I think Kent used the word suffering about 60 times last week.

38
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:27,080
Does that sound right?

39
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,900
I have some good news.

40
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:30,540
We're still going to sit in suffering.

41
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:34,100
But today, we will slowly begin to see some good news.

42
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:37,160
Some seeds of redemption in the midst of suffering.

43
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:38,500
You guys, does that sound like a plan?

44
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:39,580
All right.

45
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:40,580
Let's pray and we'll get started.

46
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:45,040
Father in heaven, you are good.

47
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:47,020
You are patient.

48
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:47,760
You are kind.

49
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:52,980
You are compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love.

50
00:04:52,980 --> 00:05:05,180
God, may we, as we sit in the story of Ruth and Naomi and Boaz this morning, may we have eyes to see all of the ways that you are bringing about redemption in the midst of our own self.

51
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:11,060
May we see that you are a God that is good and that can be trusted with our lives.

52
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:18,040
That if we would have the boldness of faith to lay down our life before you, that you will resurrect it in your life.

53
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:22,760
God, give us eyes to see, ears to hear.

54
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:26,000
In Jesus' name I pray.

55
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:26,980
Amen.

56
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:34,280
I grew up in one of those churches where we went to church three times on Sunday and once on Wednesday.

57
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:35,100
Anybody else?

58
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:37,000
Yeah, that's right.

59
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,340
I loved my church.

60
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:40,640
I enjoyed the experience that I had.

61
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:48,460
Everything except the preaching, really, was where I struggled as a kid.

62
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:53,760
I remember always trying to come up with creative ideas to avoid sitting through the sermon.

63
00:05:53,760 --> 00:06:01,360
I could still take you to my little Baptist church in Inwood, West Virginia right now and show you the best places to hide if you didn't want to be found.

64
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:09,020
I have, for the sake of the parents that have children there, I have not gone and taught a seminar for how to avoid sermons.

65
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:14,520
But I know the irony, right, the irony that I am now up here preaching today.

66
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:20,000
However, once in a while, we would have these services called testimony services.

67
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,760
And for me, I was like, all right, I can get behind this, right?

68
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:33,160
The boring preacher is going to sit down for the Sunday and the real people with the real lives, they're going to come up and they're going to talk about God's faithfulness in their lives.

69
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:37,660
I remember getting excited for those days because stories are powerful.

70
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:40,800
They have a unique way of shaping us.

71
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:45,020
They grasp our imagination in ways that a sermon never could.

72
00:06:45,020 --> 00:06:49,280
In them, we are often able to see our own stories with fresh eyes.

73
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:53,980
We can see the ways that God might be working in our lives that we don't currently have eyes to see.

74
00:06:53,980 --> 00:07:02,780
We're inspired to believe that maybe, just maybe, God might be able to use us far more than we actually ever imagined before.

75
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:08,700
And it's my opinion, and this might not be everyone's opinion, it's my opinion.

76
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:12,620
The only thing better than a personal testimony is a biographical testimony.

77
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:17,660
A testimony composed by a person who has mastered the art of storytelling.

78
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:27,240
They can take this powerful story of redemption in God's life and they can tell it in a way that gets even more into our soul.

79
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:34,460
They often have a way of narrating the story that makes us feel like we're in the room where it happened.

80
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:38,700
I'm looking for my Hamilton fans.

81
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:39,500
Anybody?

82
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:43,320
In the room where it happened.

83
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:43,880
All right, cool.

84
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:44,980
Moving on.

85
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:45,960
Nobody got that joke.

86
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:49,780
All right, you guys need to get better taste in movies, musicals.

87
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:50,460
Let's do this.

88
00:07:50,460 --> 00:07:50,820
All right.

89
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:54,840
They often make us feel like we're in the room where it happened.

90
00:07:54,840 --> 00:07:56,320
They have, thank you.

91
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:59,620
They have a way of narrating the story.

92
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:07,320
They draw out connections and foreshadow future scenes, crescendoing to the resolution in a way that we will never forget.

93
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:10,400
And this is what the book of Ruth is.

94
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:23,620
It's a biographical testimony narrated by an author who hasn't just mastered the art of storytelling, but they have been inspired by the Holy Spirit to write the very words of Scripture that God wanted preserved for us.

95
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:33,280
The reality is I know most of you will likely not remember anything I have to say, any of my main points for any length of time.

96
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:44,220
But I believe if you can get the story of Ruth's life in your heart and in your imagination, it can carry you through tremendous periods of suffering in your own life.

97
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:53,120
So my plan for today, I wish that I worked hard to try and find a creative outline with a main point and all the fun stuff that I like to do.

98
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:53,900
I just couldn't do it.

99
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:58,260
So the plan for today is we're just going to read chapter, we're just going to read chapter two verse by verse.

100
00:08:58,780 --> 00:09:04,480
I'm going to try and do my best to help you see what the author is doing with Ruth's very powerful testimony.

101
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:08,700
And hopefully through the story of Ruth, we can see our own stories with fresh eyes.

102
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:13,840
And we can see ways that God might be working in our lives that we haven't been previously aware of.

103
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:22,440
And maybe, just maybe, we can be inspired to believe that God might be able to use us in ways that we've never even imagined before.

104
00:09:22,440 --> 00:09:24,200
Sound like a plan?

105
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:25,820
I don't know why I ask you guys that.

106
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:26,560
I do it every time.

107
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:35,180
All right, one, chapter one, starting in verse 22, and then we'll do most of chapter two this morning.

108
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:40,680
Remember, where Kent left off, Ruth and Naomi are in an utterly helpless situation.

109
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:44,600
They are childless widows with no household identity in a patriarchal culture.

110
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:51,800
They are just barely surviving with probably, if they were to put odds on them surviving this suffering,

111
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:53,700
they would probably put it in the lower 10%.

112
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:55,940
They are just trying to hang on.

113
00:09:56,560 --> 00:09:59,360
With no realistic hope to get out of the situation.

114
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:01,660
They don't have any plans for how they're going to get out.

115
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:04,680
All they're trying to do is survive today.

116
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:26,440
This is the first glimpse of redemption that we have in the story, filled with suffering.

117
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:34,780
It just so happened that Ruth and Naomi happened to be coming home just at the time the barley harvest was coming.

118
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:41,840
They arrived at harvest time when food would be plentiful for the first time in a long time.

119
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:43,640
The famine has come to an end.

120
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:48,440
And not only has the famine come to an end, but they are arriving at harvest time.

121
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:52,460
And with the gleaning laws of the Old Testament that we're going to get into in a minute,

122
00:10:52,460 --> 00:11:01,280
this would give these vulnerable widows a glimmer of hope that they might just possibly have the meal that they've been longing for,

123
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:04,220
that they're desperate for, that they're in need of.

124
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:08,900
It just so happened that they arrived at the time of the barley harvest.

125
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:12,740
I want to point out that we talked about Ruth and Naomi being widows.

126
00:11:12,740 --> 00:11:16,720
But not only is Ruth a widow, she's also a foreigner and an orphan.

127
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:21,180
Kent talked last week about the importance of Ruth going back to her home

128
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:26,380
so that she could face the suffering with her real mother,

129
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:28,980
the one who would help her in her grieving process.

130
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:31,780
But she gave all of that away to follow Yahweh.

131
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:35,100
And so now Ruth is not just a widow, but she's a foreigner and an orphan.

132
00:11:35,100 --> 00:11:39,420
She is a real person, but she also functions as a literary device

133
00:11:39,420 --> 00:11:42,160
for the entire group of vulnerable people in the Old Testament.

134
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:44,640
She is a widow, a foreigner, and an orphan.

135
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:46,800
And the story of Ruth, the testimony of Ruth,

136
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:50,520
gives us God's heart for the widow, the orphan, and the foreigner,

137
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:53,360
the most vulnerable people among us.

138
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:55,640
Chapter 2, verse 1.

139
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:59,140
Now, this is weird.

140
00:11:59,140 --> 00:12:01,500
It has nothing to do with the story, seemingly, right?

141
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:04,320
Now, Naomi had a relative on her husband's side,

142
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:08,240
a man of standing from the clan of Elimelech, whose name was Boaz.

143
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:13,160
You guys ever watched a movie where they get you bought into the story of some individuals,

144
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:17,500
and then the scene cuts, and you're in a scene with a new character

145
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:20,900
that you haven't met before and seemingly has nothing to do with the characters

146
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:22,960
that the story writer got you invested in?

147
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:25,200
Thank you.

148
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:26,000
I appreciate that.

149
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,080
I have as well.

150
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:28,440
We're on the same page.

151
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:30,680
That's what the author of Ruth is doing here.

152
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:33,960
She's introducing a new character that to the original hearers,

153
00:12:33,960 --> 00:12:37,360
as they're hearing the story, this has nothing to do with the story they've got invested in,

154
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:40,540
but they're quickly going to, their paths are quickly going to cross.

155
00:12:40,540 --> 00:12:44,460
And these stories are going to be interwoven together.

156
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:52,820
Naomi has a relative who's wealthy and of high social status and character in the town of Bethlehem.

157
00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:55,400
Verse 2.

158
00:12:55,400 --> 00:13:00,100
And Ruth, the Moabite, said to Naomi,

159
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:06,780
let me go to the fields and pick up the leftover grain behind anyone whose eyes I find favor.

160
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:09,580
Now Naomi said to her, go ahead, my daughter.

161
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:15,000
So she went out and entered a field and began to glean behind the harvesters.

162
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:24,400
Ruth here shows an incredible amount of knowledge in the law of God that likely came from her living in Naomi's household for 10 years.

163
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:25,660
She wastes no time.

164
00:13:25,660 --> 00:13:33,620
She knows that the Old Testament has laws of gleaning where widows can go into the fields behind the harvesters and glean and pick up the leftovers.

165
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:37,940
The Old Testament law, and let me see, I wrote it down so that I didn't forget it.

166
00:13:37,940 --> 00:13:41,400
My notes are so small.

167
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:47,200
There it is.

168
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:56,120
Leviticus 19, Leviticus 23, and Deuteronomy 24 all dictate that the Israelite landowners are required by law not to harvest the edges of their fields

169
00:13:56,120 --> 00:13:58,940
and not to go back through the fields a second time.

170
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:06,680
The edges of the fields, the stalks of the grain dropped by the harvesters, they are all to be left for the widow, the foreigner, and the orphan.

171
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:10,340
This is a clear reminder to God's people that the land is not theirs.

172
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:15,920
It is his, and he gave it to them as a gift, and they are to steward it as if it is his and not theirs.

173
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:24,780
That he cares for all of creation, that he cares for the most vulnerable, and that his land is to be used to take care of everyone who has need.

174
00:14:26,140 --> 00:14:34,740
And so Naomi and Ruth, they know this, but what's interesting to me is that Naomi's obviously able-bodied.

175
00:14:34,740 --> 00:14:41,300
She just got back from a 50-mile journey from Moab, and yet she doesn't have the heart.

176
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:45,140
She doesn't have the hope to go out and glean.

177
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:47,480
She's sitting in the midst of her suffering.

178
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:48,620
She says, call me Mara.

179
00:14:48,620 --> 00:14:49,980
The Lord has been bitter to me.

180
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:57,020
She's sitting in her suffering, and Ruth, on behalf of Naomi, says, I'm going to go out and glean for both of us.

181
00:14:57,020 --> 00:15:06,660
I'm going to be aggressive in my gleaning to take care of both me and you, and what I need you to hear is sometimes you're in the midst of suffering so bad that you don't even have the hope to move on,

182
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:13,880
and what you need is some community around you, somebody that can see a hope for you even when you can't see a hope for yourself.

183
00:15:13,980 --> 00:15:15,920
And so Naomi doesn't have the energy.

184
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:17,620
She doesn't have the hope.

185
00:15:17,620 --> 00:15:28,580
She doesn't have the belief in God to take care of her, and so she's sitting at home struggling, wrestling with crippling depression, just ready for it to be over, and Ruth says, I've got hope enough for both.

186
00:15:28,580 --> 00:15:33,280
And so when you're in the midst of your suffering, may you surround yourself.

187
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:41,660
Even if it's somebody that's suffering alongside of you, may you surround yourself with people who can see hope for you, who can believe in hope with you.

188
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:46,200
Ruth says, I am going to go out, and I'm going to glean.

189
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:48,160
I'm going to take care of us.

190
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:53,960
I'm going to rest on the character of God, the character of God that legally demanded God's people.

191
00:15:53,960 --> 00:16:02,660
Even when they didn't have the heart of God inside of them, they still had laws commanding them to take care of the widow, the orphan, and the foreigner.

192
00:16:02,660 --> 00:16:04,900
So Ruth goes out.

193
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:08,820
She has incredible knowledge of the law.

194
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:16,380
And what's interesting here is although we don't necessarily see God actively show up in the book of Ruth until the very end,

195
00:16:16,380 --> 00:16:19,580
we don't see him doing anything crazy or miraculous to our eyes.

196
00:16:19,580 --> 00:16:22,940
We see him working through providential moments all throughout the book.

197
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:30,720
Verse 3, she goes out to glean, and she just happened to enter a field that belonged to Boaz.

198
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:34,880
Remember our wealthy relative of good standing?

199
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:42,320
It just, as it turned out, she was working in a field that belonged to Boaz, who was from the clan of Elimele.

200
00:16:43,720 --> 00:16:49,580
And the Hebrew here clearly articulates, as I think the English translation does a really good job of,

201
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:53,140
as it turned out, is very clearly saying this is not a coincidence.

202
00:16:53,140 --> 00:16:57,020
God is at work in the steps that Ruth is taking.

203
00:16:57,020 --> 00:17:03,460
He is taking her to a place where he can bring about the redemption that she's just barely holding on to hope that she might have.

204
00:17:05,380 --> 00:17:14,360
It's seemingly a coincidental encounter, but ultimately it's God working out his redemption through the kindness of people that are obedient to him.

205
00:17:14,360 --> 00:17:17,940
It's obviously divine providence.

206
00:17:17,940 --> 00:17:24,900
And again, if you are a Hebrew listener of this text for the first time, you're going to hear that phrase,

207
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:32,180
it happened as it happened, and you're immediately going to think of the first time that that was brought up in the Old Testament.

208
00:17:32,180 --> 00:17:37,040
Now, most of us, because we don't read the text in the original language and because we don't,

209
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:41,900
our whole lives aren't baked around the story of God like they were in the Old Testament, we don't think like that.

210
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:47,760
But they would have heard this phrase and they would have immediately thought of the first time it was used in the book of Genesis.

211
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:54,040
And it was used of Abraham's servant going to find a wife for Isaac.

212
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:59,800
So there's this thing in biblical interpretation called the law of firsts,

213
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:03,660
which is when a phrase or a word happens for the first time,

214
00:18:03,660 --> 00:18:07,540
it is often setting up what that word is going to mean throughout the rest of Scripture.

215
00:18:07,540 --> 00:18:11,040
Because remember, Scripture is God's grand narrative.

216
00:18:11,240 --> 00:18:17,920
It is a library of multiple books, but all of these books are one story of God's redemption from beginning to end.

217
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:21,220
And so they would have heard this and the readers of this would have thought,

218
00:18:21,220 --> 00:18:22,900
okay, this isn't possible.

219
00:18:22,900 --> 00:18:28,260
There's no way that the writer of this book is connecting a Moabite widow

220
00:18:28,260 --> 00:18:31,000
to one of the matriarchs of our nation

221
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,560
who would carry along the line of Israel.

222
00:18:33,560 --> 00:18:35,700
It's almost as if the narrator is saying,

223
00:18:35,700 --> 00:18:39,740
hey, God might be doing far more than you think he's doing in this story.

224
00:18:40,100 --> 00:18:42,620
You might, you have been hearing me tell this story about Ruth

225
00:18:42,620 --> 00:18:44,680
and you might be thinking, oh, God's going to do a great thing.

226
00:18:44,680 --> 00:18:47,180
He's going to redeem this lady's story.

227
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:48,260
He's going to help her survive.

228
00:18:48,260 --> 00:18:50,220
But no, the author is saying, what if?

229
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:53,800
What if God is so good that he's not just going to redeem her story,

230
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:56,560
but he's going to use her to redeem the whole world.

231
00:18:56,560 --> 00:19:00,820
He's going to use her to be a part of the lineage of the nation of Israel.

232
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:05,700
How audacious of a God do we serve that he takes a Moabite widow

233
00:19:05,700 --> 00:19:10,600
and brings her into the family of God and uses her in an incredible way

234
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:13,100
to bring about the line of Jesus Christ?

235
00:19:13,100 --> 00:19:22,560
Ruth and Naomi just happen to arrive at the beginning of the barley harvest,

236
00:19:22,560 --> 00:19:27,500
and Ruth just happens to stumble into Boaz Field the first day she goes to glean.

237
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:32,620
And you can almost hear the narrator telling you God is at work bringing about redemption

238
00:19:32,620 --> 00:19:33,880
in the midst of it.

239
00:19:33,880 --> 00:19:36,860
Verse 4.

240
00:19:37,980 --> 00:19:58,640
Again, we just keep falling into God's redemption plan in the life of Ruth and Naomi.

241
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:01,760
Not only do they just happen to show up at barley harvest time,

242
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:05,460
not only does she just happen to show up at Boaz's field,

243
00:20:05,520 --> 00:20:09,420
but he just happens to show up right when she gets there.

244
00:20:09,420 --> 00:20:12,860
This is the third clear evidence of God at work

245
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:15,780
in seemingly coincidental moments in the story.

246
00:20:15,780 --> 00:20:18,820
Remember, Boaz is a wealthy landowner, a man of status.

247
00:20:18,820 --> 00:20:23,160
He likely has lots of places to be and lots of things to do,

248
00:20:23,160 --> 00:20:28,920
but it just so happens that he shows up to this field when Ruth is there.

249
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:33,520
Remember week one, I told you the names are highly symbolic in the book of Ruth,

250
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:36,260
and Boaz's name is no exception to this.

251
00:20:36,260 --> 00:20:39,660
Boaz's name means in him is strength.

252
00:20:39,660 --> 00:20:44,600
And this is the play, this is the character that Boaz is going to play throughout the story,

253
00:20:44,600 --> 00:20:48,460
that he has strength, not in and of himself, but in Yahweh.

254
00:20:48,460 --> 00:20:51,840
That Boaz's strength comes from God.

255
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:54,860
And the first words out of his mouth demonstrate this.

256
00:20:55,420 --> 00:20:59,960
He greets the harvesters of his field by saying,

257
00:20:59,960 --> 00:21:02,740
the Lord be with you.

258
00:21:02,740 --> 00:21:06,000
Remember, we're set in the scene of Judges,

259
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,500
a time where everybody is turning their back on God

260
00:21:08,500 --> 00:21:10,300
and doing what is right in their own eyes.

261
00:21:10,300 --> 00:21:12,860
And yet, Boaz is a man of character.

262
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:18,560
Boaz is a man who's remained faithful in the midst of the rebellion of God's people.

263
00:21:18,660 --> 00:21:23,280
And Boaz is reminding the harvesters of his field that this field is not my field.

264
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:25,160
This field is not your field.

265
00:21:25,160 --> 00:21:28,720
This field is Yahweh's field that has been given to us as a gift.

266
00:21:28,720 --> 00:21:31,320
And we are going to steward it as if that is true.

267
00:21:33,060 --> 00:21:37,160
He greets his workers, reminding them that this is God's land

268
00:21:37,160 --> 00:21:40,660
and God is present with them as they harvest it.

269
00:21:40,660 --> 00:21:44,820
This is a field that would be very friendly to the gleaners.

270
00:21:44,820 --> 00:21:48,180
And Ruth notices it immediately.

271
00:21:48,180 --> 00:21:51,120
And he notices Ruth immediately.

272
00:21:51,120 --> 00:21:56,320
And this is where I think some of you that have heard popular versions

273
00:21:56,320 --> 00:21:57,980
of the book of Ruth being taught,

274
00:21:57,980 --> 00:22:02,280
this is where our modern American familiarity with romantic comedy shows up.

275
00:22:02,640 --> 00:22:04,800
And we just do this thing that Americans do

276
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:07,620
where we read the Scriptures as if they were written directly to us.

277
00:22:07,620 --> 00:22:13,400
And we put our culture and our storylines on their culture and their storylines.

278
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:14,780
It's very American of us to do.

279
00:22:14,780 --> 00:22:17,000
I just want to make sure we're aware of it.

280
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:20,960
And so immediately, because we're so familiar with how romantic comedies work

281
00:22:20,960 --> 00:22:22,260
in our culture, we're like,

282
00:22:22,260 --> 00:22:25,360
Oh, Boaz must be hot and bothered for Ruth, right?

283
00:22:25,360 --> 00:22:27,200
He sees her in the field.

284
00:22:27,200 --> 00:22:28,480
Who's that woman belonged to?

285
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:30,000
She's got to be good looking, right?

286
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:31,180
He's got to be interested.

287
00:22:32,220 --> 00:22:35,920
We immediately start going down this road of romance.

288
00:22:35,920 --> 00:22:40,240
But I think that's a bit of a jump.

289
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:45,340
Boaz, as a man of high standing in the nation of Israel

290
00:22:45,340 --> 00:22:48,220
and how marriage and relationships would work at that time,

291
00:22:48,220 --> 00:22:55,220
would have no interest in Ruth as a Moabite widow,

292
00:22:55,220 --> 00:22:58,780
who's likely still dressed as a mourner showing up in the field.

293
00:22:58,860 --> 00:23:00,740
And that's likely why he recognized her,

294
00:23:00,740 --> 00:23:03,280
because she's still dressed as a widow in mourning.

295
00:23:03,280 --> 00:23:07,580
And he sees a woman who hasn't been in his fields previously dressed as a widow.

296
00:23:07,580 --> 00:23:12,140
His attention towards her is likely more rooted in his generous heart for those in need.

297
00:23:12,140 --> 00:23:14,060
Now, don't worry.

298
00:23:14,060 --> 00:23:15,320
The romance will come.

299
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:22,300
But I think the romance we will see between Ruth and Boaz goes well beyond physical attraction.

300
00:23:22,300 --> 00:23:27,940
Nothing about Ruth's situation on the surface would make her a good candidate for Boaz.

301
00:23:28,480 --> 00:23:31,260
In our over-sexualized culture, listen to this.

302
00:23:31,260 --> 00:23:36,400
In our over-sexualized culture, I think we need to recapture the beauty of opposite-sex friendships

303
00:23:36,400 --> 00:23:39,160
that aren't charged by lust or selfish intention.

304
00:23:39,720 --> 00:23:41,560
We are brothers and sisters in Christ.

305
00:23:41,560 --> 00:23:48,540
And we allow ourselves to be discipled by the culture that says all male-female relationships

306
00:23:48,540 --> 00:23:52,040
is angling for some type of thing that we can get benefit out of.

307
00:23:52,040 --> 00:23:57,300
And what if we recaptured that and we began to live as if we were a family of God together?

308
00:23:57,300 --> 00:23:59,440
We need each other.

309
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:02,240
Brothers and sisters need each other.

310
00:24:02,920 --> 00:24:07,380
So I think what's going on here is a little bit deeper than just Boaz's hot and bothered for Ruth.

311
00:24:07,380 --> 00:24:09,940
All right, verse 6 and 7.

312
00:24:09,940 --> 00:24:10,600
We'll keep going.

313
00:24:10,600 --> 00:24:12,780
Oh, we're doing on time.

314
00:24:12,780 --> 00:24:13,160
Great.

315
00:24:13,160 --> 00:24:13,640
Awesome.

316
00:24:13,640 --> 00:24:14,560
The overseer replied,

317
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:16,980
She is the Moabite who came back from Moab with Naomi.

318
00:24:16,980 --> 00:24:17,480
She said,

319
00:24:17,480 --> 00:24:20,660
Please let me glean and gather among the sheaves behind the harvesters.

320
00:24:20,660 --> 00:24:23,940
She came into the field and has remained here for morning until now,

321
00:24:23,940 --> 00:24:27,060
except for a short time of rest in the shelter.

322
00:24:27,060 --> 00:24:30,280
Boaz's foreman immediately informs him,

323
00:24:30,380 --> 00:24:33,240
probably in like an awkward way of like,

324
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:37,920
this woman is asking for way more than what the law of Israel requires.

325
00:24:37,920 --> 00:24:43,900
Many scholars have said what Ruth is doing is asking for way more than just gleaning.

326
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:45,920
She's asking to go out behind the harvester.

327
00:24:45,920 --> 00:24:46,640
She's asking,

328
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:47,620
she's asking,

329
00:24:47,620 --> 00:24:49,240
she's making a bold request,

330
00:24:49,240 --> 00:24:50,240
a bold proclamation,

331
00:24:50,240 --> 00:24:53,500
a bold prayer for both her survival and for the survival of Naomi.

332
00:24:53,500 --> 00:24:53,900
She's like,

333
00:24:53,900 --> 00:24:58,420
I've got a friend who doesn't have the hope to see God's faithfulness yet,

334
00:24:58,420 --> 00:25:01,380
but I have a friend and I'm going to have hope for me and for her.

335
00:25:01,380 --> 00:25:04,380
I'm going to ask for more than just what the gleaning laws would require.

336
00:25:04,380 --> 00:25:08,280
Many scholars think that she just has no clue what's going on in the nation of Israel,

337
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:11,200
but I would argue differently.

338
00:25:11,200 --> 00:25:16,580
I think what Ruth shows is she shows an outsider's perspective on the heart of God,

339
00:25:16,580 --> 00:25:20,780
that she's heard stories of the God of Israel in light of the Moabite God.

340
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:25,600
She's heard about these laws that the God of Israel has imposed to take care of the widow,

341
00:25:25,600 --> 00:25:26,080
the orphan,

342
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:26,980
and the stranger,

343
00:25:26,980 --> 00:25:29,960
and that she is aware of God's heart for the widow,

344
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:30,880
the orphan,

345
00:25:30,880 --> 00:25:31,480
and the stranger.

346
00:25:31,480 --> 00:25:35,540
She's asking Boaz to see beyond the letter of the law to the spirit of the law.

347
00:25:35,540 --> 00:25:37,340
This is Sermon on the Mount type stuff.

348
00:25:37,340 --> 00:25:38,400
You have heard it said,

349
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:40,120
but I say to you.

350
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:43,360
Ruth wants more than religious obedience.

351
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:45,860
She's asking for sacrificial love.

352
00:25:45,860 --> 00:25:54,060
If we have any lawyers in the room this morning,

353
00:25:54,060 --> 00:25:54,600
I'm sorry.

354
00:25:54,600 --> 00:25:56,560
I'm going to take one shot and move on.

355
00:25:56,560 --> 00:26:01,420
As is the case with pretty much any written law,

356
00:26:01,420 --> 00:26:04,760
a good enough lawyer can get you out of it if they try hard enough.

357
00:26:04,760 --> 00:26:08,360
And many Jews at the time would do just that.

358
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:12,500
Many land-owning Jews would obey the very strictest,

359
00:26:12,680 --> 00:26:14,380
most ungenerous,

360
00:26:14,380 --> 00:26:16,900
literal translation of the law.

361
00:26:16,900 --> 00:26:21,240
They would ensure that the harvesters harvested as much as the field as possible,

362
00:26:21,240 --> 00:26:23,880
leaving next to nothing for the gleaners to pick up.

363
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:27,180
One scholar I read said that gleaning in many Jewish fields

364
00:26:27,180 --> 00:26:30,000
would be the financial equivalent of collecting cans to sell.

365
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:31,560
If you were lucky,

366
00:26:31,560 --> 00:26:34,360
you would work all day and just get enough for a single meal.

367
00:26:34,360 --> 00:26:36,880
And here's where I want to point out.

368
00:26:36,880 --> 00:26:41,800
Landowners who just obeyed the letter of the law

369
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:46,020
could likely be elders in their synagogues,

370
00:26:46,020 --> 00:26:48,460
but would be missing the very heart of God.

371
00:26:48,460 --> 00:26:52,620
And I wonder how many people in churches in America today

372
00:26:52,620 --> 00:26:56,160
would fall in this category where if you look at their lives,

373
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:59,760
man, they're just obedient to the cultural expectations

374
00:26:59,760 --> 00:27:01,260
of what a good Christian looks like.

375
00:27:01,260 --> 00:27:04,280
And they might find themselves in leadership positions

376
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:07,040
all around churches and doing things in the name of God,

377
00:27:07,320 --> 00:27:09,600
but what if it's just religious obedience

378
00:27:09,600 --> 00:27:12,360
and they haven't really been transformed by the heart of God?

379
00:27:12,360 --> 00:27:15,700
I've got to ask the question here in the room this morning,

380
00:27:15,700 --> 00:27:18,200
how many of us are just cultural Christians,

381
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:20,700
just doing the things that we're supposed to do,

382
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:23,500
but we haven't been captured by the generous heart of God

383
00:27:23,500 --> 00:27:25,320
for those in need around them?

384
00:27:27,540 --> 00:27:33,240
Boaz demonstrates that he has begun to grasp the heart of God,

385
00:27:33,240 --> 00:27:36,080
and Ruth has begun to grasp the heart of God,

386
00:27:36,080 --> 00:27:39,100
and she needs more than legalistic obedience to the law of God.

387
00:27:39,100 --> 00:27:43,840
She needs outrageous generosity if she's going to survive and take care of now.

388
00:27:45,760 --> 00:27:51,260
So, Boaz said to Ruth,

389
00:27:51,260 --> 00:27:52,440
my daughter, listen to me.

390
00:27:52,440 --> 00:27:54,640
Do not go and glean in another field.

391
00:27:54,640 --> 00:27:56,440
Don't go away from me.

392
00:27:56,440 --> 00:27:58,160
Stay here with the women who work for me.

393
00:27:58,160 --> 00:27:59,860
Watch the field where the men are harvesting,

394
00:27:59,860 --> 00:28:01,980
and follow along after the women.

395
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:04,200
I have told the men not to lay a hand on you,

396
00:28:04,200 --> 00:28:05,380
and whenever you are thirsty,

397
00:28:05,380 --> 00:28:08,860
get a drink from the water the men have filled.

398
00:28:08,860 --> 00:28:11,120
Boaz responds with the tremendous generosity

399
00:28:11,120 --> 00:28:12,620
that we would expect of a person

400
00:28:12,620 --> 00:28:16,620
who has experienced the radical grace and mercy of God.

401
00:28:16,620 --> 00:28:18,080
Not only does he permit her request,

402
00:28:18,080 --> 00:28:19,460
but he goes above and beyond,

403
00:28:19,460 --> 00:28:21,080
inviting her to drink fresh water

404
00:28:21,080 --> 00:28:24,040
from the water that his employees have drawn,

405
00:28:24,040 --> 00:28:27,000
promising her safety.

406
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,060
And many scholars think when he says,

407
00:28:29,060 --> 00:28:30,180
watch the fields,

408
00:28:30,180 --> 00:28:35,080
that he's actually offering her continued gleaning rights,

409
00:28:35,080 --> 00:28:36,360
not just in his barley harvest,

410
00:28:36,360 --> 00:28:37,760
but in the wheat harvest and beyond.

411
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:38,780
He's saying,

412
00:28:38,780 --> 00:28:41,100
you stay with me for as long as you need to stay with me.

413
00:28:41,460 --> 00:28:42,640
You will be taken care of,

414
00:28:42,640 --> 00:28:43,620
and you will be safe.

415
00:28:43,620 --> 00:28:46,200
To read this as Boaz being generous

416
00:28:46,200 --> 00:28:48,320
and hopes to have a physical relationship with Ruth

417
00:28:48,320 --> 00:28:50,780
is to miss his embodiment of the law of God.

418
00:28:50,780 --> 00:28:53,680
Boaz is demonstrating a love of God

419
00:28:53,680 --> 00:28:54,580
and a love of neighbor,

420
00:28:54,580 --> 00:28:56,920
which all of the law is wrapped up in.

421
00:28:56,920 --> 00:29:01,720
All of God's law is not wrapped up

422
00:29:01,720 --> 00:29:04,820
in following it strictly to obedience,

423
00:29:04,820 --> 00:29:06,300
getting a lawyer, getting a pastor,

424
00:29:06,300 --> 00:29:07,420
getting a preacher to tell you

425
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:09,100
that you can just do this, this, or this.

426
00:29:09,640 --> 00:29:12,240
All of the law is wrapped up in these two commandments.

427
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:14,100
Love the Lord your God with all of your heart,

428
00:29:14,100 --> 00:29:15,460
soul, mind, and strength,

429
00:29:15,460 --> 00:29:17,820
and love your neighbor as yourself.

430
00:29:17,820 --> 00:29:20,700
Verse 10.

431
00:29:20,700 --> 00:29:23,300
At this, she bowed down with her face to the ground,

432
00:29:23,300 --> 00:29:24,120
and she asked him,

433
00:29:24,200 --> 00:29:26,640
why have I found such favor in your eyes

434
00:29:26,640 --> 00:29:29,000
that you would notice me, listen, a foreigner?

435
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:34,640
Ruth went into this request

436
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:36,020
knowing it was a bold request,

437
00:29:36,020 --> 00:29:40,040
likely fearful that she was not only gonna get shot down,

438
00:29:40,040 --> 00:29:42,060
but she was gonna get kicked out of the field altogether.

439
00:29:42,060 --> 00:29:44,440
She was making a bold, bold request,

440
00:29:44,440 --> 00:29:46,880
and Boaz takes her request and doubles down,

441
00:29:46,880 --> 00:29:48,680
and we're gonna see him later even triple down,

442
00:29:48,740 --> 00:29:50,880
and she says, why have you done this?

443
00:29:50,880 --> 00:29:52,260
She's shocked, and she's surprised.

444
00:29:52,260 --> 00:29:53,220
I'm a foreigner.

445
00:29:53,220 --> 00:29:59,440
He wants to know why Boaz is treating her like this,

446
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:01,240
and he gives us the answer, and guess what?

447
00:30:01,240 --> 00:30:04,560
The answer is not, I saw you in the field,

448
00:30:04,560 --> 00:30:05,760
and you looked kind of hot.

449
00:30:05,760 --> 00:30:10,220
All right, 11 and 12.

450
00:30:10,340 --> 00:30:37,360
His response shows,

451
00:30:38,340 --> 00:30:41,060
she says, why have you seen me?

452
00:30:41,060 --> 00:30:42,260
I'm a foreigner.

453
00:30:42,260 --> 00:30:44,820
And his response shows,

454
00:30:44,820 --> 00:30:47,600
essentially, I think that he's affirming

455
00:30:47,600 --> 00:30:49,660
her conversion into the nation of Israel.

456
00:30:49,660 --> 00:30:52,660
We said that when she turned her back on Moab,

457
00:30:52,660 --> 00:30:53,720
and when she told Naomi,

458
00:30:53,720 --> 00:30:55,060
your God is my God,

459
00:30:55,060 --> 00:30:56,460
that that was her conversion moment.

460
00:30:56,460 --> 00:30:58,380
She's saying, I am going to live,

461
00:30:58,380 --> 00:31:01,020
surrendered to the people of Israel.

462
00:31:01,020 --> 00:31:02,100
And to this point,

463
00:31:02,100 --> 00:31:03,800
it looks like everybody is still treating her

464
00:31:03,800 --> 00:31:05,500
like a Moabite woman,

465
00:31:05,500 --> 00:31:08,920
but Boaz sees the conversion that happened on the road.

466
00:31:08,920 --> 00:31:11,520
He sees that she is now surrendered to Yahweh,

467
00:31:11,520 --> 00:31:13,860
and not only does he give her physical provision,

468
00:31:13,860 --> 00:31:17,080
but he gives her spiritual acceptance into the community.

469
00:31:17,080 --> 00:31:20,360
He essentially affirms her conversion

470
00:31:20,360 --> 00:31:21,460
on the road to Bethlehem,

471
00:31:21,460 --> 00:31:23,760
and welcomes her as a convert into the nation of Israel.

472
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:25,800
He doesn't just give her physical provision,

473
00:31:25,800 --> 00:31:27,240
but communal fellowship.

474
00:31:28,380 --> 00:31:30,980
And look, he then prays a prayer of blessing over her.

475
00:31:30,980 --> 00:31:34,100
He wants her to experience the provision of Yahweh

476
00:31:34,100 --> 00:31:39,060
that encouraged her to leave everything behind to follow him.

477
00:31:39,060 --> 00:31:41,140
But notice that he doesn't just pray for her

478
00:31:41,140 --> 00:31:42,080
to be blessed by God.

479
00:31:42,080 --> 00:31:44,900
He views himself as an instrument

480
00:31:44,900 --> 00:31:46,640
in the hands of Yahweh's provision.

481
00:31:46,640 --> 00:31:49,380
He doesn't just ask God to bless her,

482
00:31:49,380 --> 00:31:52,000
but he blesses her in the name of God.

483
00:31:52,900 --> 00:31:55,160
This is what it looks like to follow God.

484
00:31:55,160 --> 00:31:55,780
Not just to say,

485
00:31:55,780 --> 00:31:57,120
I hope you get better.

486
00:31:57,120 --> 00:31:58,440
I hope God takes care of you.

487
00:31:58,440 --> 00:31:59,240
I'm praying for you.

488
00:31:59,240 --> 00:32:01,080
No, it's faith in action.

489
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:02,840
It's prayer and blessing.

490
00:32:02,840 --> 00:32:04,280
This is James 2.

491
00:32:04,280 --> 00:32:05,360
What good is our faith

492
00:32:05,360 --> 00:32:07,420
if we just pray for people and wish them well,

493
00:32:07,420 --> 00:32:10,800
but do nothing actually to help them in their time of need?

494
00:32:10,800 --> 00:32:13,680
And one of the major themes of the book of Ruth,

495
00:32:13,680 --> 00:32:15,600
and a major theme of this message,

496
00:32:15,600 --> 00:32:17,360
is God's redemptive work

497
00:32:17,360 --> 00:32:19,760
often includes the participation of these people.

498
00:32:19,760 --> 00:32:22,020
We are invited to pray,

499
00:32:22,020 --> 00:32:23,140
may your kingdom come

500
00:32:23,140 --> 00:32:25,100
and your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.

501
00:32:25,100 --> 00:32:27,660
And then we are called to participate in that prayer,

502
00:32:27,660 --> 00:32:30,740
cultivating God's kingdom on earth as it is in heaven,

503
00:32:30,740 --> 00:32:34,820
doing his will in our lives and to those around us.

504
00:32:34,820 --> 00:32:39,440
Boaz prays a blessing over Ruth,

505
00:32:39,440 --> 00:32:42,340
but then he becomes a blessing to Ruth.

506
00:32:42,340 --> 00:32:43,160
Verse 13,

507
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:46,860
may I continue to find favor in your eyes, my Lord,

508
00:32:46,940 --> 00:32:47,300
she said,

509
00:32:47,300 --> 00:32:49,120
you have put me at ease by spreading,

510
00:32:49,120 --> 00:32:51,180
by speaking kindly to your servant,

511
00:32:51,180 --> 00:32:54,180
though I do not have standing as one of your servant.

512
00:32:54,180 --> 00:32:56,920
Ruth perceives the message,

513
00:32:56,920 --> 00:32:59,940
receives the message with overwhelming gratitude.

514
00:32:59,940 --> 00:33:03,020
This doesn't take away any of her previous sufferings,

515
00:33:03,020 --> 00:33:05,840
but it's a glimpse of God's redemptive work

516
00:33:05,840 --> 00:33:06,860
in the midst of her suffering.

517
00:33:06,860 --> 00:33:08,220
I wanna point this out.

518
00:33:08,220 --> 00:33:10,140
Oftentimes in American Christianity,

519
00:33:10,140 --> 00:33:10,480
we're like,

520
00:33:10,480 --> 00:33:12,400
well, yeah, you were suffering,

521
00:33:12,400 --> 00:33:13,640
but look, God's blessing you now.

522
00:33:13,640 --> 00:33:14,060
It's like, no,

523
00:33:14,060 --> 00:33:16,860
like we can sit in our suffering in the past,

524
00:33:17,340 --> 00:33:18,880
the goodness of God in our lives

525
00:33:18,880 --> 00:33:21,800
doesn't have to make us pretend like the suffering isn't there.

526
00:33:21,800 --> 00:33:26,940
We can continue to wrestle with the ways in which we've suffered

527
00:33:26,940 --> 00:33:29,060
and yet continue to move towards God

528
00:33:29,060 --> 00:33:30,660
as we receive his blessing.

529
00:33:30,660 --> 00:33:32,520
Verse 14,

530
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:35,760
at mealtime is where it gets good.

531
00:33:35,760 --> 00:33:37,760
Boaz said to her,

532
00:33:37,760 --> 00:33:38,660
come over here,

533
00:33:38,660 --> 00:33:40,720
have some bread and dip it in the wine and vinegar.

534
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:42,360
When she sat down with the harvesters,

535
00:33:42,360 --> 00:33:44,060
he offered some of the roasted grains.

536
00:33:44,060 --> 00:33:45,480
She ate all that she wanted

537
00:33:45,480 --> 00:33:47,680
and had some left over.

538
00:33:47,680 --> 00:33:50,500
Boaz triples down on his generosity,

539
00:33:50,500 --> 00:33:52,540
including her in table fellowship,

540
00:33:52,540 --> 00:33:55,080
which means everything in this culture.

541
00:33:55,080 --> 00:33:55,500
He says,

542
00:33:55,580 --> 00:33:57,840
you belong here as a part of my family.

543
00:33:57,840 --> 00:34:00,340
You have a spot to sit at the table.

544
00:34:00,340 --> 00:34:01,820
This is a huge deal in their culture

545
00:34:01,820 --> 00:34:03,460
and it's a huge deal in our culture.

546
00:34:03,460 --> 00:34:05,760
It's one thing to serve food at a soup kitchen.

547
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:07,680
It's entirely different altogether

548
00:34:07,680 --> 00:34:08,860
to grab a bowl yourself

549
00:34:08,860 --> 00:34:11,240
and sit down next to the person that you're serving.

550
00:34:11,240 --> 00:34:14,940
Boaz is treating Ruth like a part of the family,

551
00:34:14,940 --> 00:34:17,320
even though she has no family.

552
00:34:17,320 --> 00:34:19,720
This is the kingdom of God.

553
00:34:20,620 --> 00:34:22,340
The generosity of God

554
00:34:22,340 --> 00:34:24,800
lived out through one faithful, obedient person.

555
00:34:24,800 --> 00:34:25,720
Is Boaz perfect?

556
00:34:25,720 --> 00:34:26,740
Absolutely not.

557
00:34:26,740 --> 00:34:30,080
But he is faithfully obeying the heart of God,

558
00:34:30,080 --> 00:34:32,300
the widow, the orphan, and the foreigner.

559
00:34:32,300 --> 00:34:35,040
Ruth, who is likely running on fumes

560
00:34:35,040 --> 00:34:37,100
from a 50-mile journey with no food,

561
00:34:37,100 --> 00:34:39,220
now has a hot meal prepared for her.

562
00:34:39,220 --> 00:34:42,200
And that's no small thing.

563
00:34:42,200 --> 00:34:43,620
Like she's gleaning, that's great,

564
00:34:43,620 --> 00:34:47,160
but that seed is gonna have to be harvested.

565
00:34:47,160 --> 00:34:49,300
It's gonna take a while for a real meal

566
00:34:49,300 --> 00:34:50,940
to come out of the thing that she's gleaning.

567
00:34:50,940 --> 00:34:53,920
And so Boaz gives her a warm meal.

568
00:34:53,920 --> 00:34:57,720
It says she ate all that she could eat

569
00:34:57,720 --> 00:34:59,300
and had some left over.

570
00:34:59,300 --> 00:35:00,880
Remember I talked about the Bible

571
00:35:00,880 --> 00:35:02,540
being one grand narrative?

572
00:35:02,540 --> 00:35:04,620
All of the books of the Bible together

573
00:35:04,620 --> 00:35:05,600
are telling one story.

574
00:35:05,600 --> 00:35:06,740
Can you think of a time

575
00:35:06,740 --> 00:35:08,480
where you've heard that phrase being used?

576
00:35:08,480 --> 00:35:11,820
Ate all they could eat and had some left over?

577
00:35:11,820 --> 00:35:15,160
Loaves and fish, right?

578
00:35:15,160 --> 00:35:16,360
Same phrase.

579
00:35:16,360 --> 00:35:18,000
Two miracles in the book of Matthew.

580
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:20,360
Feeding of the 5,000, the feeding of the 4,000.

581
00:35:20,360 --> 00:35:22,420
The feeding of the 5,000

582
00:35:22,420 --> 00:35:24,560
is a predominantly Jewish group of people.

583
00:35:24,560 --> 00:35:27,260
They eat all they can eat

584
00:35:27,260 --> 00:35:28,560
and have some left over.

585
00:35:28,560 --> 00:35:31,180
How many baskets of food they have left over?

586
00:35:31,180 --> 00:35:31,720
Anybody remember?

587
00:35:31,720 --> 00:35:33,120
12.

588
00:35:33,120 --> 00:35:36,420
We got 5,000 predominantly Jewish people.

589
00:35:36,420 --> 00:35:38,200
They have 12 baskets left over.

590
00:35:38,200 --> 00:35:40,860
12 as a representative of all the nation of Israel.

591
00:35:40,860 --> 00:35:42,340
Jesus is saying with his miracle,

592
00:35:42,340 --> 00:35:44,340
there is more than enough physical

593
00:35:44,340 --> 00:35:46,520
and spiritual provision for the nation of Israel.

594
00:35:46,520 --> 00:35:48,400
He doubles down two chapters later

595
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:50,080
and feeds a group of 4,000 people,

596
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:52,280
which were predominantly Gentile people.

597
00:35:52,280 --> 00:35:53,840
They ate all they wanted

598
00:35:53,840 --> 00:35:55,920
and had seven baskets left over.

599
00:35:55,920 --> 00:35:58,820
Seven is the number that represents all of creation.

600
00:35:58,820 --> 00:36:02,060
Jesus is saying in Ruth here and in Matthew there,

601
00:36:02,060 --> 00:36:03,660
there is more than enough physical

602
00:36:03,660 --> 00:36:06,400
and spiritual provision for Jews and for Gentiles.

603
00:36:06,400 --> 00:36:07,960
Everything is God's.

604
00:36:08,160 --> 00:36:10,260
And God cares for all of creation

605
00:36:10,260 --> 00:36:12,280
and there is more than enough to go around.

606
00:36:12,280 --> 00:36:15,640
And the more people that acknowledge that reality

607
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:16,460
and live into it,

608
00:36:16,460 --> 00:36:19,020
the less widows, orphans, and foreigners will go hungry.

609
00:36:19,020 --> 00:36:21,860
Or you could say it like this,

610
00:36:21,860 --> 00:36:24,180
the more that God's people embody God's presence

611
00:36:24,180 --> 00:36:25,380
through sacrificial love,

612
00:36:25,380 --> 00:36:27,820
the more clearly we will see heaven come to earth.

613
00:36:29,900 --> 00:36:31,660
And many of us opt out of this

614
00:36:31,660 --> 00:36:33,160
because we don't have as much as Boaz.

615
00:36:33,160 --> 00:36:35,620
We play the comparison game.

616
00:36:35,620 --> 00:36:37,020
We say, well, he's rich, he's wealthy,

617
00:36:37,020 --> 00:36:38,060
he's got social status.

618
00:36:38,060 --> 00:36:41,180
But what we don't see is that Ruth

619
00:36:41,180 --> 00:36:42,800
is being that same blessing to Naomi

620
00:36:42,800 --> 00:36:44,240
even though she has nothing in her.

621
00:36:47,960 --> 00:36:49,760
We can embody the kingdom of God

622
00:36:49,760 --> 00:36:52,320
whether we're a widow with nothing in our hands

623
00:36:52,320 --> 00:36:55,060
or a business owner with a lot in our hands.

624
00:36:55,060 --> 00:36:58,080
God has not called us to worry about what's in our hands.

625
00:36:58,080 --> 00:37:00,220
He's called us to trust him with what's in our hands

626
00:37:00,220 --> 00:37:01,840
and steward it to those around us

627
00:37:01,840 --> 00:37:04,680
in a way that embodies the kingdom of God

628
00:37:04,680 --> 00:37:06,520
on earth as it is in heaven.

629
00:37:06,520 --> 00:37:11,060
This story is gonna climax in a love story

630
00:37:11,060 --> 00:37:12,220
between Ruth and Boaz,

631
00:37:12,220 --> 00:37:14,080
but the underlying theme of the whole book

632
00:37:14,080 --> 00:37:16,340
is a love story between God and his people

633
00:37:16,340 --> 00:37:19,040
and the redemption that he can bring

634
00:37:19,040 --> 00:37:20,820
if we would love him with our whole being

635
00:37:20,820 --> 00:37:23,500
and love our neighbors as ourselves.

636
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:25,440
We gotta get going.

637
00:37:25,440 --> 00:37:26,580
15 and 16.

638
00:37:26,580 --> 00:37:30,400
As she got up to glean,

639
00:37:30,400 --> 00:37:31,940
Boaz gave orders to his men,

640
00:37:31,940 --> 00:37:33,460
let her gather among the sheaves

641
00:37:33,460 --> 00:37:34,480
and don't reprimand her,

642
00:37:34,480 --> 00:37:37,520
even pull out some of the stocks for her

643
00:37:37,520 --> 00:37:38,560
from among the bundles

644
00:37:38,560 --> 00:37:40,140
and leave them for her to pick up

645
00:37:40,140 --> 00:37:41,240
and do not rebuke her.

646
00:37:41,240 --> 00:37:43,500
So Ruth gleaned in the field until evening.

647
00:37:43,500 --> 00:37:45,980
Then she threshed the barley

648
00:37:45,980 --> 00:37:46,660
she had gathered

649
00:37:46,660 --> 00:37:48,240
and then it amounted to about an ephah.

650
00:37:48,240 --> 00:37:52,200
Boaz triples down on his generosity,

651
00:37:52,200 --> 00:37:54,200
ensuring that Ruth will be treated well

652
00:37:54,200 --> 00:37:56,480
and that her and Naomi will be provided for

653
00:37:56,480 --> 00:37:59,120
above and beyond what a gleaner could ever imagine.

654
00:37:59,120 --> 00:38:01,100
This is grace.

655
00:38:01,100 --> 00:38:05,000
Grace is God treating us like we don't deserve

656
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:06,220
and could never pay back.

657
00:38:06,220 --> 00:38:08,660
This is Boaz demonstrating the grace of God

658
00:38:08,660 --> 00:38:09,360
to Ruth and Naomi,

659
00:38:09,360 --> 00:38:11,600
giving them far more than they could ever work for,

660
00:38:11,600 --> 00:38:14,280
far more than they could ever pay him back for.

661
00:38:15,460 --> 00:38:17,360
Just as we see Ruth is a real person

662
00:38:17,360 --> 00:38:19,140
who functions as a literary device

663
00:38:19,140 --> 00:38:21,040
representing the most vulnerable among us,

664
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:23,680
Boaz is a literary foreshadowing of Christ

665
00:38:23,680 --> 00:38:25,440
who will provide not just physical redemption

666
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:26,560
but spiritual redemption

667
00:38:26,560 --> 00:38:28,360
to all who will cling to him in faith.

668
00:38:28,360 --> 00:38:31,460
The story of Ruth is the story of the whole Bible

669
00:38:31,460 --> 00:38:33,260
told in four chapters and three characters.

670
00:38:33,260 --> 00:38:36,860
Ruth returns home

671
00:38:36,860 --> 00:38:39,460
to what I would assume is a very anxious,

672
00:38:39,460 --> 00:38:40,760
stressed out Naomi

673
00:38:40,760 --> 00:38:42,260
because she knows how dangerous

674
00:38:42,260 --> 00:38:43,480
gleaning in the fields can be,

675
00:38:43,480 --> 00:38:45,040
especially as a foreigner.

676
00:38:45,040 --> 00:38:47,960
She can't imagine what could have happened to Ruth.

677
00:38:47,960 --> 00:38:50,480
So Ruth gleans and heads home.

678
00:38:50,480 --> 00:38:54,360
She carries back a tremendous amount of grain

679
00:38:54,360 --> 00:38:56,800
back to her mother-in-law.

680
00:38:56,800 --> 00:38:58,120
Let's see here.

681
00:38:58,120 --> 00:38:59,320
Ruth also brought,

682
00:38:59,320 --> 00:39:01,920
where are we at?

683
00:39:01,920 --> 00:39:02,340
Verse 18.

684
00:39:02,440 --> 00:39:03,400
She carried it back to town

685
00:39:03,400 --> 00:39:04,540
and her mother-in-law saw

686
00:39:04,540 --> 00:39:05,540
how much she had gathered.

687
00:39:05,540 --> 00:39:06,900
Ruth also brought out

688
00:39:06,900 --> 00:39:08,500
and gave her what she had left over

689
00:39:08,500 --> 00:39:10,020
from the lunch that she had eaten.

690
00:39:10,020 --> 00:39:11,800
Her mother-in-law asked her,

691
00:39:11,800 --> 00:39:12,920
where did you glean today

692
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:13,940
and where did you work?

693
00:39:13,940 --> 00:39:15,040
Blessed is the man

694
00:39:15,040 --> 00:39:17,040
who took notice on you.

695
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:24,180
Ifa is what most scholars would argue

696
00:39:24,180 --> 00:39:25,700
about a half-month wages

697
00:39:25,700 --> 00:39:27,360
of a legitimate worker in the field.

698
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:29,520
So now you've got a gleaner who,

699
00:39:29,520 --> 00:39:32,100
and imagine how Boaz's workers

700
00:39:32,100 --> 00:39:32,740
would have felt

701
00:39:32,740 --> 00:39:35,300
as they see Ruth packing up

702
00:39:35,300 --> 00:39:37,020
her two-weeks paycheck

703
00:39:37,020 --> 00:39:38,360
from one day of work.

704
00:39:38,360 --> 00:39:41,220
This is the kingdom of God.

705
00:39:41,220 --> 00:39:43,400
It's not up to us

706
00:39:43,400 --> 00:39:45,180
the way that God stewards

707
00:39:45,180 --> 00:39:46,560
what he has given others.

708
00:39:49,020 --> 00:39:50,140
So Ruth heads home

709
00:39:50,140 --> 00:39:51,980
with about a half-month wages

710
00:39:51,980 --> 00:39:52,860
and a warm meal.

711
00:39:52,860 --> 00:39:54,500
Again, think about how impactful

712
00:39:54,500 --> 00:39:55,780
this would be for Naomi.

713
00:39:55,780 --> 00:39:56,700
She doesn't have to wait

714
00:39:56,700 --> 00:39:57,060
a couple,

715
00:39:57,060 --> 00:39:58,060
she doesn't have to wait

716
00:39:58,060 --> 00:39:58,920
a long period of time

717
00:39:58,920 --> 00:40:00,260
for them to get the grain

718
00:40:00,260 --> 00:40:00,980
and harvest it

719
00:40:00,980 --> 00:40:01,620
and roast it

720
00:40:01,620 --> 00:40:02,340
and make a meal.

721
00:40:02,340 --> 00:40:03,420
She has a warm meal

722
00:40:03,420 --> 00:40:04,440
waiting for her,

723
00:40:04,440 --> 00:40:05,140
something Naomi

724
00:40:05,140 --> 00:40:06,260
would be desperate for.

725
00:40:06,260 --> 00:40:09,020
Naomi is thrilled

726
00:40:09,020 --> 00:40:10,820
to hear about this chance encounter,

727
00:40:10,820 --> 00:40:11,900
which is probably how Naomi

728
00:40:11,900 --> 00:40:12,680
is still viewing it

